e1::a "\"Nàlǐ shì wēixiǎn dìdài\", suǒyǐ tāmen yuēhǎo le, wúlùn yùjiàn shénme wēixiǎn, liǎng gè rén dōu yào xiānghù bāngzhù." ::e
t2::a A merchant and a farmer travelled together. They needed to pass through an uninhabited forest. ::t
t3::a A merchant and a farmer were travelling together. They needed to pass through a forest with no trace/whiff/mist of people. ::t
t4::a "After the bear approached, it saw the farmer lying on the ground. It came over to sniff him with its nose and circled around him for a long time." ::t
t5::a "After the bear got close, it saw the farmer lying on the ground and came over to sniff using its nose, circling around him for a long time." ::t
t6::a "But the farmer hugged the tree trunk and just couldn't climb up, so he had no choice but to quickly lie on the ground and pretend to be dead." ::t
t7::a "But the farmer hugging the tree just couldn't climb up, he was forced to quickly lie on the ground and play dead." ::t
u8::a Bù jǐn bù慢 de shuō ::?tc
t9::a "It was a dangerous area, so they made an agreement that no matter what danger they encountered, both of them would help each other." ::t
t10::a It was a dangerous place so they made a pact that no matter what danger they experienced they would help each other. ::t
t11::a "Knowing that the bear had gone, the merchant only then came down from up in the tree. the farmer also got up. The merchant then asked the farmer: \"Friend, when the great bear was leaning over you just now, what did he whisper in your ear?\"" ::t
t12::a "Not long after they walked into the forest, danger arrived—a big bear was walking toward them from up ahead." ::t
t13::a "Not until the big bear had walked far away did the merchant come down from the tree, and the farmer climbed up from the ground too. The merchant then asked the farmer, \"My friend, what did the big bear whisper right next to your ear just now?\"" ::t
u14::a Nàlǐ shì wéixiǎn dìdài ::?tc
t15::a Nàlǐ shì wēixiǎn dìdài ::t
u16::a Nǐ de ěr páng gēn nǐ shuōle xiē shénme ya ::?tc
t17::a "Pretending not to care at all, the farmer said calmly and unhurriedly, \"The big bear didn't actually tell me any secrets. It only told me not to make friends with untrustworthy people next time!\"" ::t
t18::a "Seeing the bear walking toward them, the merchant abandoned the farmer and climbed the tree alone to hide." ::t
e19::a "Shāngrén dòngzuò mǐnjié, tā gǎnkuài páshàng zuìjìn de dà shù duǒ qǐlái." ::e
t20::a "Speaking in a measured, unhurried manner" ::t
t21::a "The bear thought the farmer was truly a dead person, so it walked away. Because bears do not eat dead people." ::t
t22::a "The farmer closed his eyes tightly, held his breath, and kept perfectly still." ::t
t23::a "The farmer shut his eyes tightly, held his breath, and did not move at all." ::t
t24::a "The farmer, pretending not to care, evenly replied: \"Actually, the great bear didn't speak any secrets, he only told me that next time don't make friends with untrustworthy people.\"" ::t
t25::a "The great bear presumed the farmer really was a dead person, and soon walked away, because bears be like don't eat dead people yo!" ::t
t26::a "The merchant acted with agility, quickly climbing the nearest large tree to hide." ::t
t27::a The merchant moved quickly and nimbly; he immediately climbed up the nearest large tree to hide. ::t
t28::a They hadn't gone far through the forest when danger suddenly was upon them. A large bear was walking towards them from up ahead. ::t
t29::a "This sentence contains two \"lin\" words. In the first half of the sentence, lín is the character 林.  The Word: 树林 i.e. shùlín with literal meaning 树 /shù / tree + 林 / lín / forest/grove.  Character Origin: the character 林, it is literally made of two \"tree\" characters 木 = mù standing side-by-side. In the second half of the sentence, lín is the character 临 [traditional: 臨]. The Word: 来临 / láilín has literal meaning 来 / lái / to come + 临 / to arrive/draw near. Usage: 临 on its own means \"to be close to,\" \"to face,\" or \"to arrive.\" When combined as 来临, it means \"to arrive\" or \"to approach.\" It is highly formal and usually used for abstract things, e.g., danger approaching, winter arriving, or a new era coming." ::t
e30::a "Tāmen zǒujìn shùlín bù jiǔ, wēixiǎn jiù láilín le —— yì zhī dà xióng cóng qiánmiàn cháo tā men zǒu lái." ::e "This sentence contains two \"lin\" words. In the first half of the sentence, lín is the character 林.  The Word: 树林 i.e. shùlín with literal meaning 树 /shù / tree + 林 / lín / forest/grove.  Character Origin: the character 林, it is literally made of two \"tree\" characters 木 = mù standing side-by-side. In the second half of the sentence, lín is the character 临 [traditional: 臨]. The Word: 来临 / láilín has literal meaning 来 / lái / to come + 临 / to arrive/draw near. Usage: 临 on its own means \"to be close to,\" \"to face,\" or \"to arrive.\" When combined as 来临, it means \"to arrive\" or \"to approach.\" It is highly formal and usually used for abstract things, e.g., danger approaching, winter arriving, or a new era coming. · This sentence contains two \"jiu\" words: 不久 /bù jiǔ / not long after, and 就 / jiù / then or immediately." ::tip
t31::a What was whispered in your ear? ::t
e32::a "Yí wèi shāngrén hé yí wèi nóngfū jiébàn tóngxíng, tāmen yào jīngguò \"yí gè méiyǒurén yān de shùlín\"." ::e
